  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (3x² + z²)/2, drawn at half height. It is steeper along x, so overshoot appears there first: steps along x grow once the learning rate passes 2/3.",
    "Mini-batch slope = true slope + noise with spread 2·√((64−B)/(63·B)): largest at B = 1, exactly zero at B = 64. A step lowers the loss only on average.",
    "Orange arrow: the next step, −learning rate × noisy slope. Grey arrow: −learning rate × true slope. Both are drawn at 2× their true length; the readout gives the true length of the orange step.",
    "A run ends after 40 steps (finished) or when the loss passes 6, the loss at the bowl's steep edge x = ±2 (diverged). Off-bowl positions are drawn at the edge; the loss readout keeps the true value.",
    "Noise comes from a fixed sequence. A new run continues the sequence, so its noise differs; Reset replays it from the start. Trails hold the start point plus up to 40 steps."
  ]
}